SELECT c.slug,'Government',
 'Texas Awards More Than $22 Million for Panhandle Electric Reliability',
 'Two June 2026 grants support North Plains Electric Cooperative transmission and substation projects outside ERCOT; this historical news URL has been transparently reconstructed.',
 NULL::text,'Keep TX Red Newsroom','Office of the Texas Governor','https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-announces-more-than-22-million-in-grants-for-electric-reliability-in-northwest-texas',
 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/file/Houston_Lighting_%26_Power_Substation%2C_Quitman_at_Maury%2C_Houston%2C_Texas_0409111304BW_%285605879839%29.jpg','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/file/Houston_Lighting_%26_Power_Substation%2C_Quitman_at_Maury%2C_Houston%2C_Texas_0409111304BW_%285605879839%29.jpg',
 '2011 archival photo of a Houston electrical substation; illustrative Texas electric infrastructure, not North Plains Electric Cooperative or the June 2026 grant projects.',
 'Patrick Feller, Houston Lighting & Power Substation at Quitman and Maury, photographed April 9, 2011. Wikimedia Commons CC BY 2.0 https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/ . Houston infrastructure image is not the funded Panhandle facility.',
 '2026-06-28T12:00:00Z'::timestamptz,'news','{"updated":"2026-10-14T18:00:00Z","intro":["Governor Greg Abbott announced two Texas Energy Fund grants totaling more than $22 million on June 24, 2026, for electric system improvements by North Plains Electric Cooperative in the northeastern Texas Panhandle. The state said the projects would increase substation capacity, add transmission connections and improve the cooperative’s ability to restore service after problems. The money is aimed at infrastructure serving rural communities outside the main ERCOT grid.","This reconstruction preserves a Keep TX Red news address dating to June 28, 2026. The original editorial copy was lost during earlier newsroom data changes, so the details below are checked against the governor’s release and the Public Utility Commission’s Texas Energy Fund program information rather than represented as recovered original wording."],"sections":[{"heading":"Which communities are affected","paragraphs":["North Plains Electric Cooperative serves parts of the northeastern Panhandle. The governor identified portions of Hansford, Hutchinson, Lipscomb, Ochiltree and Roberts counties as beneficiaries of the grant-supported work. The announcement concerns the co-op’s service territory, not the entire Texas electric system and not a promise that every resident in those counties will see an immediate change to monthly bills.","Reliability investments matter especially where long distances and smaller customer populations make the economics of power delivery different from those of major cities. A transmission line or critical transformer failure may interrupt power across a broad geographic area. Building additional connections and improving capacity can give operators more options to move power or repair damaged equipment."]},{"heading":"What the two projects are supposed to build","paragraphs":["The largest project includes about 14 miles of new or upgraded transmission-line sections and switching equipment along existing corridors. According to the governor’s June 24 announcement, these changes are intended to strengthen service reliability and give the cooperative more operational flexibility while replacing older components.","The second project expands the Ochiltree Interchange so that a spare transformer can be brought into operation. A spare transformer is useful because major power transformers are costly, difficult to replace quickly and important to moving energy safely between parts of the network. Bringing one into service increases capacity and can improve redundancy, although the precise benefit depends on system design and operating conditions."]},{"heading":"Why the grant comes from an Outside ERCOT program","paragraphs":["The Texas Energy Fund includes multiple financing programs with different purposes. The grants in this announcement are part of the Outside ERCOT Grant Program, administered by the Public Utility Commission of Texas. Its eligibility and funding rules focus on qualifying electric infrastructure and generating facilities in Texas regions not served by the Electric Reliability Council of Texas power region.","The PUCT program describes eligible uses such as modernization, weatherization and reliability work on transmission and distribution networks. This differs from the fund’s In-ERCOT Generation Loan Program, which helps finance additional dispatchable generation inside ERCOT. Conflating the two programs can give readers the wrong impression about which utilities and customers receive support."]},{"heading":"What grid reliability means in practical terms","paragraphs":["Substations change voltage levels and help distribute electricity from transmission systems to customers. Switching equipment lets operators isolate sections, reroute flows where the network supports it and conduct maintenance. Transmission lines connect sources and substations. When an investment expands capacity and adds alternate routes, it can reduce the number of ways a single equipment failure disrupts service.","None of that eliminates every outage. Severe storms, wildfire, extreme temperature, equipment problems and vegetation can still damage wires and interrupt service. The projects should be judged against engineering milestones, completion dates and measurable service reliability rather than assuming that receiving grant funding means the infrastructure has already been built."]},{"heading":"Funding announcement versus completed construction","paragraphs":["The June 24 release announced grant support and described planned improvements. It did not provide evidence that all 14 miles of line work had already been completed by the time of the announcement. Grant execution, permitting, procurement and construction can occur on different schedules; verified reports from the cooperative and PUCT are more reliable measures of actual project completion than a headline about money being awarded.","The governor’s office quoted cooperative General Manager Mike Wolfe describing the value of additional transmission connections for member reliability. His statement expresses the cooperative’s expectations. A future independent evaluation would examine how projects performed in service, whether outage duration decreased and how the cooperative accounted for the public funds."]},{"heading":"What customers can check and how this history was restored","paragraphs":["Customers can consult North Plains Electric Cooperative for service and outage information and the Texas Energy Fund’s official site for grant-program requirements. Public Utility Commission filings and performance reports can provide additional information where available. Individual Texans should not assume the grant changes their electric provider, service territory or retail rate.","The original Keep TX Red report at this June 28 address no longer survives as an article record. This page was reconstructed October 14, 2026 from verifiable government information and retains the historical URL with that fact disclosed. The accompanying photograph shows a substation in Houston, photographed in 2011, and is an illustrative Texas electric-infrastructure image—not a photograph of North Plains Electric’s work or the grant ceremony."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How much did Texas announce for North Plains Electric Cooperative?","a":"More than $22 million in two Texas Energy Fund grants was announced on June 24, 2026, for infrastructure serving parts of the northeastern Texas Panhandle."},{"q":"Which counties were named in the announcement?","a":"The governor identified portions of Hansford, Hutchinson, Lipscomb, Ochiltree and Roberts counties within North Plains Electric Cooperative territory."},{"q":"Were the projects already finished when the grants were announced?","a":"The governor’s announcement described proposed and grant-supported improvements; it did not establish that all construction was complete."},{"q":"Is this funding part of ERCOT generation loans?","a":"No. The grants are administered through the Texas Energy Fund’s Outside ERCOT Grant Program for qualifying infrastructure outside the ERCOT region."}],"sources":[{"label":"Office of Governor Greg Abbott — June 24, 2026 North Plains grants","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-announces-more-than-22-million-in-grants-for-electric-reliability-in-northwest-texas"},{"label":"Texas Energy Fund / PUCT — Outside ERCOT Grant Program and eligibility","url":"https://www.txenergyfund.texas.gov/Outside-ERCOT-Grants"},{"label":"Texas Energy Fund / PUCT — overview of financing programs","url":"https://www.txenergyfund.texas.gov/"},{"label":"Wikimedia Commons — Patrick Feller licensed Houston power substation photograph","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houston_Lighting_%26_Power_Substation%2C_Quitman_at_Maury%2C_Houston%2C_Texas_0409111304BW_%285605879839%29.jpg"}],"keyTakeaways":["Texas announced more than $22 million for two reliability projects at North Plains Electric Cooperative.","The upgrades include about 14 miles of transmission-line improvements and work at the Ochiltree Interchange.","The funding comes from the Outside ERCOT Grant Program, not a loan program for ERCOT generation."]}'::jsonb,
